{{!--  Inline-comments migration template  ----------------------------------}}

You will receive **one complete React file**.

For every hard-coded UI string you migrate to Tolgee **always**:

1. Replace the literal with `<T keyName="…"/>` or `t('…')`.
2. Insert **exactly one** JSX review-comment that:
   • is a single line in the form  
     `{/* {"description":"…","default":"…"} */}`  
   • sits **directly above the Tolgee call** it documents  
   • is never placed on a wrapper element.

------------------------------------------------------------------
PLACEMENT RULES
------------------------------------------------------------------
1. JSX syntax only – use `{/* … */}`, never block comments.  
2. **One line only** – the whole JSON object stays on a single line.  
3. Allowed positions  

| Situation | Where the comment must be placed |
|-----------|----------------------------------|
| Stand-alone element (`<button>`, `<label>`, etc.) | Comment on the line **just above** that element. |
| `<T …/>` nested inside JSX | Comment on the line **just above** the **parent** tag. |
| `t('…')` inside any prop list | Comment as the **first child inside** the element body (after the opening tag). |
| Top-level `return (` | **Write any needed comment above the `return` line itself**, never between `return (` and the first tag. |

4. **Never** place a comment  
   • between `return(` and the first tag  
   • between attributes in an opening tag  
   • above JS/TS declarations (`const …`, `function …`)  

**Very important**: If there is a return statement, the comment must be placed **above the return statement**.
**Very important**: No comments should be placed inside jsx wrappers**.
**Very important**: If there are form options with hard-coded strings, make sure there are comments for all form options and placed exactly above the options**.
**Very important**: If there are Table rows with hard-coded strings, make sure there are comments for all table rows and placed exactly above the table rows**.
**Very Important**: You should generate comments that are valid codes at all times**.

**Always follow the placement rules exactly.**

5. **One comment per element**  
6. Code must compile (`npm run build` or `tsc`).  

------------------------------------------------------------------
GOOD / BAD EXAMPLES
------------------------------------------------------------------
✅ GOOD  – multi-prop element
<Layout title={t('title')} description={t('desc')}>
{/* {"description":"Layout title + desc","default":"Original text"} */}
…
</Layout>

❌ BAD – comment between props
<Layout
{/* {"description":"bad"} */} // ✗ not allowed
title={t('title')}

❌ BAD – comment floats after `return(`
return ( // comment must be above this line
{/* bad */} // ✗
<Layout …>

